1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Bian Lan 卞蘭 (ca. 230), Late Eastern Han, early Wei period writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bian Lan’s natal place was Kaiyang 開陽 in Langye 琅邪 (modern Linyi 臨沂, Shandong). He was the son of the Bian Bing 卞秉, younger brother of Cao Cao’s wife Lady Bian 卞 (160–230). During the Wei he earned the favor of Cao Pi 曹丕 (187–226)
2 CONC  by presenting him with a ''fu'' praising Cao Pi’s virtue. He continued to serve in the court under Emperor Ming 明 (r. 226–239). Bian Lan is primarily known as a ''fu'' writer. In 232, Bian Lan wrote “Xuchang gong fu” 許昌宮賦 (''Fu''
2 CONC  on the Xuchang Palace) to celebrate the construction of a new palace in Xu. Known for his outspokenness, he often admonished Emperor Ming on his extravagance.
2 CONT
2 CONT The monographs on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' and the two Tang histories list Bian Lan’s collected works in two ''juan''. This was lost in the Song. Yan Kejun has collected four of his prose pieces in the ''Quan shanggu Sandai
2 CONC  Qin Han Sanguo Liuchao wen''.
2 CONT
2 CONT Not Specified Wu Jie
2 CONT
2 CONT Citation
2 CONT * 卞蘭 Bian Lan, Brill’s Chinese Reference Shelf. Brill Online, 2017. http://chinesereferenceshelf.brillonline.com/ancient-literature/entries/SIM-300021
