1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Cao Jiong''' 曹冏 (fl. 243), ''zi'' '''Yuanshou''' 元首. Three States (Wei) period writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cao Jiong’s ancestral home was Qiao 譙 in Pei 沛 kingdom (modern Bo 亳 county, Anhui). He was a descendant of Cao Shuxing 曹叔興, the elder brother of Cao Cao’s 曹操 (155–220) grandfather Cao Teng 曹騰 (d. ca. 158), and the great uncle of C
2 CONC ao Fang 曹芳 (231–274), the Prince of Qi 齊. In 243, Cao Jiong presented a petition to Cao Fang arguing that the Wei had failed to follow the method that previous ruling houses used to solidify their power, namely awarding fiefs to b
2 CONC lood kin. Cao Jiong complains that imperial kinsmen have been installed in small estates and have little power. He urges that the best way to strengthen the Cao ruling house is to “show affection to its relatives” by enfeoffing th
2 CONC em in strategic places that will serve as a counter-balance to the non-imperial relatives such as the Sima clan. Cao Jiong’s essay is a review of the enfeoffment systems of the Xia, Yin, Zhou, Qin, and Han, as well as the recent W
2 CONC ei. Thus, the title given to this work in the ''Wen xuan'' is “Liu dai lun” 六代論 (Disquisition on the six dynasties). According to the ''Weishi Chunqiu'', because of Cao Fang’s youth, his intended recipient of the petition was the 
2 CONC regent Cao Shuang 曹爽 (d. 249).
2 CONT
2 CONT This Cao Jiong should not be confused with the Cao Jiong (d. 226) who was the son of Cao Rui 曹叡 (206–239; alt. 205–239), Emperor Ming of Wei.
