1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Xiao Hui 蕭撝 (515–573), zi Zhixia 智霞. Liang and Northern Zhou period writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiao Hui's ancestral home was Nan Lanling 南蘭陵 (the Danyang 丹陽 area northwest of modern Changzhou, Jiangsu). He was a son of Xiao Xiu  蕭秀 (475–518), a younger brother of Emperor Wu of the Liang (502–549). During the early Liang he 
2 CONC received the noble title Marquis of Yongfeng  永豐 county. At the age of twelve he began study at the national university. He read widely in the Classics and histories and became an accomplished writer. His first official position w
2 CONC as palace steward. He then served on the staff of the crown prince Xiao Gang 蕭綱 (503–551) as librarian and secretary. In 537, emissaries from the Eastern Wei, Li Xie 李諧 (496–544) and Lu Yuanming 盧元明 (n.d.), visited the Liang court
2 CONC . Emperor Wu (r. 502– 549) considered Xiao Hui's oral eloquence “remarkable,” and appointed him vice director of the Secretariat in which capacity he received the emissaries' gifts in the guest lodge. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiao Hui later was sent out as governor of Songning 宋寧 (modern Chengdu) and Songxing 宋興 (north of modern Chengdu), followed by postings as governor of Baxi 巴西 (east of modern Mianyang 綿陽, Sich-uan) and Zitong 梓潼 (east of modern Mi
2 CONC anyang, Sichuan). When the Hou Jing rebellion began in 548, Emperor Wu's eighth son Xiao Ji 蕭紀 (508–553) seized control of Yizhou 益州 (modern Sichuan). He appointed Xiao Hui to several military positions and put him in charge of mi
2 CONC litary defense in Yizhou. On 16 May 552, Xiao Ji proclaimed himself emperor in Chengdu. He appointed Xiao Hui secretariat director. While Xiao Ji led his army eastward down the Yangzi River, he had Xiao Hui defend Chengdu. Seeing 
2 CONC that Yizhou was in a weakened state, Yuwen Tai 宇文泰 (507–556), the de facto ruler of the Western Wei, sent the general Yuchi Jiong 尉遲迥 (d. 580) to lead an army against Xiao Hui. The Western Wei forces seized Chengdu on 1 September 
2 CONC 553, and Xiao Hui surrendered. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiao Hui joined the Western Wei court where he served as palace attendant, cavalry general-in-chief, and was granted the honorific title of Commander Unequalled in Honor. When the Northern Zhou replaced the Western Wei in 557, Xia
2 CONC o Hui was granted the noble title of Duke of Huangtai 黃臺 commandery. In 560, the Northern Zhou ruler Emperor Wu (r. 560–578) commanded scholars to collate the Classics and histories in the Linzhi Hall. Xiao Hui participated in the
2 CONC  compilation of the ''Shi pu'' 世譜 (Geneaology of the eras), a record of the reigns of rulers from Fuxi to the end of the Western Wei. In 567, Xiao Hui was assigned as regional inspector of Shangzhou 上州 (northeast of modern Yunxi 鄖
2 CONC 西, Hubei). He returned to the imperial court as instructor and professor in the national university. In 571, Xiao Hui was appointed junior guardian, and was made junior tutor in 572. In this year he also received the title of Duke
2 CONC  of Caiyang 蔡陽 commandery. Xiao Hui died in 573 at the age of fifty-nine. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiao Hui was a skilled calligrapher in the cursive and clerical styles. He also had some knowledge of mathematics and medicine. According to his biography in the ''Zhou shu'' he had written some “several ten thousand words of poet
2 CONC ry, ''fu'', and diverse prose pieces.” The monograph on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' lists Xiao Hui's collected works in ten ''juan''. Both Tang histories record a collection of the same size. This was lost in the Song. Lu Qinl
2 CONC i has collected six of his poems in ''Xian Qin Han Wei Jin Nanbeichao shi''. Yan Kejun includes only one prose piece in ''Quan shanggu Sandai Qin Han Sanguo Liuchao wen''.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng and Shen Yucheng, Zhongguo wenxuejia dacidian, 385.
2 CONT
2 CONT DRK
