1 DEAT
2 DATE 581
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Xiao Dayuan 蕭大圜 (d. 581), ''zi'' Rengxian 仁顯. Liang and Northern Zhou literatus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiao Dayuan's ancestral home was Nan Lanling 南蘭陵 (the Danyang 丹陽 area northwest of modern Changzhou). He was the twentieth son of Xiao Gang 蕭綱 (503–551), Emperor Jianwen 簡文 (r. 549–551) of the Liang dynasty. According to his biogr
2 CONC aphy in the ''Zhou shu'', he was a child prodigy who could recite the “San du fu” 三都賦 (''Fu'' on the Three Capitals), ''Xiao jing'', and ''Lun yu'' when he was only four years old. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 550, Xiao Dayuan was granted the title Prince of Leliang commandery 樂梁郡 (administrative seat, southeast of modern Xiangzhou  象州, Guangxi). He was named General of Manifest Benevolence and was appointed governor of Danyang 丹陽, w
2 CONC hich administered the capital area of Jiankang. Emperor Jianwen was killed during the Hou Jing rebellion of 548–552. Xiao Dayuan fled into hiding. After the rebellion was put down, he returned to Jiankang 建康 (modern Nanjing 南京, Ji
2 CONC angsu) where he found refuge in the Shanjue 善覺 Monastery. After General Wang Sengbian 王僧辯 (d. 555) learned about Xiao Dayuan's plight, he provided Xiao Dayuan with a boat and provisions and sent him to Jiangling 江陵 (modern Jiangli
2 CONC ng, Hubei). Xiao Dayuan's uncle Xiao Yi 蕭繹 (508–554), who just succeeded to the Liang throne, was very pleased to see him. He granted him the title Prince of Jinxi commandery 晉熙郡 (modern Deyang 德陽, Sichuan) and appointed him Gener
2 CONC al Who Pacifies the Distance and governor of Langye 琅邪 (southwest of modern Lianyun 連雲, Jiangsu) and Pengcheng 彭城 (modern Xuzhou 徐州, Jiangsu). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fearing that human affairs were often unpredictable, Xiao Dayuan decided to detach himself from most of his relatives and friends. He had only two or three close associates. He communicated with his sisters and brothers only in wr
2 CONC iting. He focused on the study of the Songs, Rites, Documents, and Changes. Emperor Yuan once asked Xiao Dayuan about several dozen passages in the Five Classics. Xiao Dayuan was able to answer his queries concisely and clearly wi
2 CONC thout hesitation. Emperor Yuan marveled at his profound and broad understanding. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In November 554, a Western Wei army led by General Yu Jin 于謹 (493– 568) launched an attack on Jiangling. Emperor Yuan assigned Xiao Dayuan to assist his brother Xiao Dafeng 蕭大封 (fl. 552–580) to sue for peace with Yu Jin. Their mis
2 CONC sion failed, and Emperor Yuan was captured and killed on 27 January 555. The Western Wei installed Xiao Cha 蕭詧 (519–562) as a puppet emperor in Jiangliang. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 555, Xiao Dayuan moved to Chang'an where he had been taken as a captive of the Western Wei. Yuwen Tai 宇文泰 (505–556), the founder of the Northern Zhou dynasty (posthumous title Emperor Wen 文), treated him with great courtesy. In
2 CONC  562, Emperor Wu 武 (r. 560–578) of the Northern Zhou enfeoffed Xiao Dayuan as Duke of Shining 始寧 county (administrative headquarters in southeast of modern Bazhong 巴中, Sichuan). Soon thereafter he named him General-in-chief of Cha
2 CONC riot and Horse and granted him the honorary title Unequaled in Honor. Xiao Dayuan was selected as an academician in the Linzhi dian 麟趾殿 (Hall of the Unicorn's Foot). The imperial archives in Chang'an had acquired a copy of the ''L
2 CONC iang Wudi ji'' 梁武帝集 (Collected writings of Emperor Wu of Liang) in forty ''juan'' and the ''Jianwen ji'' 簡文集 (Collected writings of Emperor Jianwen) in ninety ''juan''. It was not until then that Xiao Dayuan had the opportunity to
2 CONC  view these two collections. He took a year's time to make careful hand-copies of both works. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiao Dayuan believed in Buddhist karma, and he wrote a long essay titled “Xianfang zhi yan” 閑放之言 (Words of easeful abandon) in which he argues for the benefits of a relaxed and carefree lifestyle. In 575, he was appointed companio
2 CONC n to Yuwen You 宇文逌 (d. 580), Prince of Teng 滕. Yuwen You had strong interests in scholarly matters, and he once queried Xiao Dayuan about whether it was true that Xiao Yi had written a history of the Liang. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Sui replaced the Northern Zhou in 581, Xiao Dayuan took up posts at the Sui court. He was first appointed director of the Secretariat. He later was assigned as governor of Xihe 西河 commandery (administrative headquarters i
2 CONC n modern Linfen 臨汾, Shanxi). He died shortly after taking up this appointment. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiao Dayuan's biography in the ''Zhou shu'' mentions that he compiled the ''Liang jiushi'' 梁舊事 (Precedents of the Liang) in thirty ''juan'', ''Yu ji'' 寓記 (Notes on my temporary lodging?) in three ''juan'', ''Shi sangyi zhu'' 士喪儀注 
2 CONC (Commentary to mourning rites for scholars) in five ''juan'', and ''Yaojue'' 要訣 (Essential solutions?) in two ''juan''. It also says that his collected works consisted of twenty ''juan''. None of these works has survived. His only
2 CONC  extant writings are “Zhuhua fu” 竹花賦 (''Fu'' on bamboo flowers) and the “Xianfang zhi yan” mentioned above, which is included in his biography in the ''Zhou shu''.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng and Shen Yucheng, Zhongguo wenxuejia dacidian, 378. 
2 CONT * Yu Ouyang 于歐洋 and Cao Shujie 曹書杰. “Nanchao huangshi wenxue wenxian kaocha” 南朝皇室文學文獻考察. Guji zhengli yanjiu xuekan (2012: 3): 50–59.
2 CONT
