1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Xiao Yuanzheng 蕭圓正 (d. 553), ''zi'' Mingyun 明允. Liang period literatus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xiao Yuanzheng's ancestral home was Nan Lanling 南蘭陵 (northwest of modern Changzhou 常州, Jiangsu). He was the second son of Xiao Ji 蕭紀 (508–553). He was handsome, a skilled conversationalist, and had a good relationship with other l
2 CONC iterati. He was enfeoffed as Marquis of Jiang'an 江安. After Emperor Wu of Liang (r. 502–549) died at the hands of Hou Jing in 549, he joined with a thousand Liang courtiers to urge Xiao Yi 蕭繹 (508–555) to assume control over the Li
2 CONC ang state. When Xiao Ji established his regime in Shu, Xiao Yuanzheng secretly plotted to join him, but was captured by Xiao Yi. After Xiao Ji was killed in battle, Xiao Yi ordered Xiao Yuanzheng to commit suicide. Refusing to eat
2 CONC , he died in prison in 530 about the age of thirty. Four lines of a linked verse poem he composed in prison are preserved in the ''Nan shi''.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng and Shen Yucheng, Zhongguo wenxuejia dacidian, 393.
2 CONT
