2 DATE 464
1 DEAT
2 DATE 499
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Xie Tiao''' 謝朓 (464–499), ''zi'' '''Xuanhui''' 玄暉. Southern Qi writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Tiao's ancestral home was Yangjia 陽夏 in Chen 陳 commandery (modern Taikang 太康, Henan). Xie Tiao's family home was near Jiankang. He served as governor of Xuancheng 宣城 and thus he also is known as Xie Xuancheng  謝宣城. He was dist
2 CONC antly related to Xie Lingyun 謝靈運 (385–433), and is thus called Xiao Xie 小謝 (Younger Xie). Although he came from a distinguished clan, the family status had declined considerably by the time of Xie's birth. Xie Tiao's grandfather, 
2 CONC Xie Shu 謝述 (390–435), was governor of Wuxing 吳興, and his grandmother was the elder sister of Fan Ye 范曄 (398–445). In 445, Fan Ye led a failed insurrection to overthrow Emperor Wen (r. 424–453) and was executed. Xie's uncles, Xie Z
2 CONC ong 謝綜 (b. ca. 415) and Xie Yue 謝約 (b. ca. 423), who participated in the insurrection, also were executed. Xie's father, Xie Wei 謝緯 (b. 430), was not one of the conspirators and escaped execution because he was married to Princess
2 CONC  Changcheng 長城, the fifth daughter of Emperor Wen. However, he was punished by being exiled to Guangzhou 廣州 (modern Guangdong). He did not return to the north for thirteen years (until 457). Xie Tiao was born in the north, in a su
2 CONC burb of the capital. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Tiao was a diligent student and became well known as a writer. During his youth, he witnessed the decline of the Song house and the rise of the Qi. Because he was close to the imperial family, he must have had intimate knowled
2 CONC ge of the coups and assassinations that occurred with great frequency during this period. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Tiao entered government service in 482 at the age of nineteen. His first position was as acting aide on the staff of the defender-in-chief, Xiao Ni 蕭嶷 (444–492), Prince of Yuzhang 豫章, who was the second son of Xiao Daocheng 蕭道
2 CONC 成 (427–482, the Qi founder. Xie held this position for two or three years. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 486, Xie Tiao joined the staff of another prince, Xiao Zilong 蕭子隆  (474–494), Prince of Sui 隨, who was the eighth son of Xiao Ze 蕭賾, Emperor Wu, r. 483–493. Xiao Zilong was in charge of a large portion of the southeast, includi
2 CONC ng Guiji 會稽 (modern Shaoxing) and Yongjia 永嘉 (modern Wenzhou). During this time, Xie Tiao was a regular participant in the gatherings hosted by Xiao Ziliang 蕭子良 (460–494), Prince of Jingling. Not all members of the prince's litera
2 CONC ry salon were from his personal staff. They included men such as Xie Tiao, who held positions elsewhere. Xie Tiao's works from this period are mainly occasional pieces written on command or in response to pieces by other members o
2 CONC f Xiao Ziliang's salon. Among these works are two ''fu'': “Gao song fu” 高松賦 (''Fu'' on the tall pine), composed to match a ''fu'' on the same subject by Xiao Ziliang himself; and “Ni Song Yu feng fu” 擬宋玉風賦 (Imitating Song Yu's ''F
2 CONC u'' on the wind), written upon command by Xiao Ziliang. During this period Xie Tiao wrote a ten-part poem, each in four lines, the “Yongming yue” 永明樂 (Music on the Age of Eternal Brilliance), a ceremonial hymn in praise of the Yon
2 CONC gming reign. Xie Tiao left Xiao Zilong's service in 488 and joined the staff of Wang Jian 王儉 (452–489), who was one of the most powerful and most respected officials during the Song and early Qi period. His daughter was a concubin
2 CONC e of Xiao Zilong, and thus he and Xiao Zilong were close friends. In the early Qi, Wang Jian supervised the preparation of a catalogue of the imperial library and headed the central administration. He also was tutor for the heir d
2 CONC esignate and the sons of nobility. In 489, Xie Tiao was named secretary on the staff of the heir designate Xiao Changmao 蕭長懋 (458–493). 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 491, Xie Tiao returned to the entourage of Xiao Zilong, Prince of Sui. From 491 to autumn of 493, he resided in Jiangling 江陵, where Xiao Zilong was serving as regional inspector of Jingzhou 荊州 and General Defending the West. As
2 CONC  he was leaving the capital to embark on the journey south, Xie wrote an eight-line poem, “Jiang fa Shitou shang fenghuo lou” 將發石頭上烽火樓 (About to depart from Shitou, I climb the beacon tower): 
2 CONT
2 CONT In Jingzhou, Xie Tiao soon became Xiao Zilong's favorite. Xie's position rose to that of ''wenxue'' or instructor. His colleagues included Yu Wuling 庾於陵 (470?–517?) and Zong Guai 宗夬 (456–504). Xiao frequently held gatherings at wh
2 CONC ich members of his staff wrote poems together. In autumn of 493, Wang Xiuzhi 王秀之 (442–494), a senior administrator on Xiao Zilong's staff, reported to the emperor that Xie Tiao was too intimate with Zilong and should be recalled t
2 CONC o the capital. The emperor immediately ordered his return. After he arrived in the capital, Xie wrote a long letter to Xiao Zilong, expressing his friendship and affection. 
2 CONT
2 CONT One of Xie Tiao's most famous poems comes from this period. Just before reaching the capital, he wrote “Zan shi xiadu ye fa Xinlin zhi Jingyi zeng Xifu tongliao” 暫使下都夜發新林至京邑贈西府同僚 (After temporarily serving in the lower capital, I 
2 CONC depart at night from Xinlin and upon reaching the capital city, I present a poem to my fellow officials in the Western Bureau). Xie presented the poem to his colleagues in Jiangling (the “lower capital), the location of the Wester
2 CONC n Bureau. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after Xie Tiao returned to the capital, Emperor Wu died. Xiao Zhaoye 蕭昭業 (473–494), the eldest son of Xiao Changmao (the heir designate who had died in 493), succeeded him. The person who had de facto control of the admini
2 CONC stration was Xiao Daocheng's nephew, Xiao Luan 蕭鑾 (459–498), posthumously known as Emperor Ming (r. 494–498). In 494 Xiao Luan deposed Xiao Zhaoye and put a puppet emperor on the throne, Xiao Zhaowen 蕭昭文 (484–494). Xie Tiao served
2 CONC  as adviser and secretary to Xiao Luan. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie's service to Xiao Luan contrasted dramatically with the actions of his friend Wang Rong 王融 (468–494). When Emperor Wu was on his deathbed, some members of the court sought to have Xiao Ziliang named heir. The leader of this gr
2 CONC oup was Wang Rong who was only twenty-five years of age, but held command of an army that was to be sent into battle against the Northern Wei invaders. Wang recruited a small group of soldiers and occupied the palace of the deceas
2 CONC ed heir designate, Xiao Changmao, and attempted to have Xiao Ziliang named heir. However, just before he died, Emperor Wu named his grandson, Xiao Zhaoye, his successor. Wang Rong was arrested and ordered to commit suicide. In mid
2 CONC -May of 494 Xiao Ziliang “died of grief.” 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 495, Xiao Luan deposed Xiao Zhaowen and installed himself as emperor. He is known by his posthumous title of Donghun hou 東昏侯 (r. 498–501). He appointed Xie Tiao as assistant director of the imperial library and promoted him to 
2 CONC director of the Secretariat. Xie wrote some of the important petitions for Xiao Luan, including the petition urging Xiao Luan to accede to the throne. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the summer of 495, Xie Tiao was sent out to serve as governor of Xuancheng 宣城 (near modern Xuancheng, Anhui). The administrative center of Xuancheng was at Yuanling 宛陵, which was only about seventy-five kilometers south of the 
2 CONC capital. Xie Tiao seemed to be happy about leaving the turmoil of the capital. In a poem he wrote on the way to Xuancheng, “Zhi Xuancheng jun chu Xinlin pu xiang Banqiao” 之宣城郡出新林浦向板橋 (On the way to Xuancheng commandery, I head tow
2 CONC ard Banqiao from Xinlin Ford), he mentions how he eagerly anticipates the quiet reclusion he can find in this post. 
2 CONT
2 CONT While in Xuancheng, Xie Tiao wrote a number of landscape poems. Unlike Xie Lingyun, however, his landscape verse is not philosophical. He does not use the landscape as an image of the Buddhist absolute or to ponder the question of
2 CONC  immortality. A good example is his “You Jingting shan” 遊敬亭山 (Wandering Mount Jingting), which he wrote about Jingting Mountain located ten ''li'' north of Xuancheng. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 497, Xie Tiao returned to the capital where he assumed his former position in the Secretariat. Later, he joined the staff of Xiao Baoyi 蕭寶義  (n.d.), prince of Jin'an 晉安 in Nan Xuzhou 南徐州 (administrative seat, Jingkou 京口, modern
2 CONC  Zhenjiang). In the fourth month of 498 Xie's father-in-law Wang Jingze 王敬則 (435–498) was planning a revolt against Emperor Ming. Instead of joining Wang Jingze, Xie Tiao informed on him. When the revolt was put down, Xie was rewa
2 CONC rded with the title of ''shangshu libu lang'' 尚書吏部郎 (director of the Ministry of Personnel in Department of State Affairs). Xie is thus sometimes called Xie Libu 謝吏部 (Xie Minister of Personnel). 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 499, Jiang Shi 江祏 (d. 499) and his brother Jiang Si 江祀 (maternal cousins of Emperor Ming), along with several others, plotted to set up Xiao Yaoguang 蕭遙光 (468–499), Prince of Shi'an 始安, as emperor. They attempted to enlist Xie 
2 CONC Tiao in their group. When Xie refused, Xiao Yaoguang had Xie put into prison where he died at the age of thirty-six. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The earliest known collection of Xie Tiao's works is the ''Xie Tiao ji'' 謝朓集  (Collected works of Xie Tiao) in twelve ''juan'' listed in the monograph on bibliography of the ''Sui shu''. The ''Sui shu'' also lists a ''Xie Tiao yij
2 CONC i'' 謝朓逸集 (Collection of fragments of Xiao Tiao) in one ''juan''. The bibliography monographs of the ''Jiu Tang shu'' and ''Xin Tang shu'' both record a ''Xie Tiao ji'' in ten ''juan''. The Song official catalogue ''Chongwen zongmu
2 CONC '' 崇文總目 records a ten-''juan'' collection under the title ''Xie Xuanhui ji'' 謝玄暉集 (Collected works of Xie Tiao). The bibliography chapter of the ''Tong zhi'' 通志 by Zheng Qiao 鄭樵 (1104–1162) lists three collections: ''Xie Tiao ji''
2 CONC  in twelve ''juan'', a ''Waiji'' 外集 in one ''juan'', and a ''Xie Tiao yiji'' 謝朓逸集 in one ''juan''. The earliest extant edition of Xie's collected works is the ''Xie Xuancheng ji'' 謝宣城集 in five ''juan'' with a preface dated 1157 by
2 CONC  Lou Zhao 樓炤 (1131–1163). Lou Zhao only included the first five ''juan'' of the ten ''juan'' version that consisted of ''fu'', musical pieces, and 102 poems. He excluded the pieces in the last five ''juan'' on the grounds that the
2 CONC y were “pragmatic works” that concerned matters of a decadent age. Lou Zhao's version is mentioned in the ''Zhizhai shulu jieti'' 直齋書錄解題 of Chen Zhensun 陳振孫 (ca. 1190–post 1249) and the ''Wenxian tongkao'' 文獻通考 of Ma Duanlin 馬端臨 (
2 CONC 1254–1325).
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Collections
2 CONT
