1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Xie An 謝安 (320–385), ''zi'' Anshi 安石, Eastern Jin statesman, thinker, and writer. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie An's ancestral home was Yangjia 陽夏 in Chen 陳 commandery (modern Taikang 太康, Henan). He was the third son of Xie Pou 謝裒 (fl. 336) who served during the Eastern Jin as palace attendant, minister of the Ministry of Personnel, and
2 CONC  administrator of the princedom of Wu 吳. For the first forty years of his life, Xie An refused to pursue an official career. He also declined numerous invitations to take office. He spent most of his time engaged in conversations 
2 CONC with like-minded friends. Known for his quick wit, he became much admired by Wang Meng 王濛 (309–47) and Wang Dao 王導 (276–339). Ca. 339, he married the younger sister of Liu Tan  劉惔 (d. 347), who was a famous conversationalist of th
2 CONC e Eastern Jin. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie An took up residence in Guiji, where he wandered the hills fishing and fowling with the Buddhist monk Zhi Dun 支遁 (314–366), the calligrapher Wang Xizhi 王羲之 (321–379, alt. 303–361), and the recluse-poet Xu Xun 許詢 (fl. ca. 358).
2 CONC  When they returned from their outings, they engaged in conversation and wrote literary pieces. It was said “they never had any intention of dwelling in the profane world.” In 353, Xie An participated in the famous third day third
2 CONC  month gathering hosted by Wang Xizhi at Lan ting. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie An's elder brother Xie Yi 謝奕 (d. 358) and younger brother Xie Wan 謝萬 (d, 361) both had successful careers already in the 350s. Xie An did not take office until 360, when he was forty years old. His first position was assistant
2 CONC  to Huan Wen 桓溫 (312–373) who held de facto power at the Eastern Jin court. Huan Wen held Xie An in the highest regard. In 361, Xie An's brother Xie Wan died of illness, and Xie An resigned from office and returned home. In 363, h
2 CONC e was appointed governor of Wuxing 吳興 (administrative seat Wucheng 烏程, south of modern Wuxing, Zhejiang). In 372, he was summoned to the imperial court where he was appointed palace attendant. At this time Emperor Jianwen (r. 372)
2 CONC  became gravely ill, and Huan Wen was plotting to seize the imperial throne. Xie An, Wang Tanzhi 王坦之 (330–375), and Wang Biaozhi 王彪之 (305–377) opposed him. Huan Wen hosted a feast at Xinting 新亭 southwest of Jiankang at which he st
2 CONC ationed a military guard that was under orders to kill Xie An and Wang Tanzhi. During the festivities Xie An showed no change in mood or appearance, and his relaxed composure frightened Huan Wen, who commanded the military guard t
2 CONC o withdraw. He proceeded to laugh and drink with Xie and Wang for the entire day. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Some time after Huan Wen's death in 374, Xie An was appointed vice director of the Department of State Affairs. He was subsequently assigned as regional inspector of Yangzhou 揚州 (administrative seat, Moling 秣陵, modern Nanjing). At
2 CONC  this time Xie An and his nephew Xie Xuan 謝玄 (343–388) organized the Beifu bing 北府兵 (Northern garrison troops), which was an army led almost exclusively by members of the Xie clan. Xie Xuan was appointed regional inspector of Yanz
2 CONC hou 兗州 and put in charge of military affairs north of the Yangzi. His headquarters was in Guangling 廣陵. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 376, Xie An was appointed overseer of Imperial Secretariat affairs, followed by an assignment in 377 as minister of education. In 378, he oversaw the construction of a new palace in Jiankang. In 381, he played a leading role in
2 CONC  the establishment of the national university. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 383, the Former Qin ruler Fu Jian 苻堅 (338–385) led a large army south across the Huai River with the goal of conquering the Eastern Jin. Xie An sent seven armies against him, including one led by Xie Xuan, and another led by hi
2 CONC s younger brother Xie Shi 謝石 (327–388). The Jin forces defeated Fu Jian's army at the Fei River 淝水 near Shouyang 壽陽 (modern Shouchun, Anhui). As a reward for achieving this victory, Xie An was granted the prestigious title of gran
2 CONC d guardian (''taibao'' 太保). He presented a petition to the court requesting to lead a military expedition to recover the lost northern realm. Meanwhile, the Former Qin regime of Fu Jian had become severely weakened by defections o
2 CONC f members of the Murong 慕容 and Yao 姚 clans. After achieving some initial success in the northern campaign, the Eastern Jin army suffered a crushing defeat on 4 May 385 at Wuqiaoze 五橋澤 north of modern Guangzong 廣宗, Hebei. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, Sima Daozi 司馬道子 (364–402), the Prince of Guiji, had seized power at the Eastern Jin court. Rather than challenge him, Xie An retreated to Guangling 廣陵 (modern Yangzhou) where he died on 10 October 385 at the age of sixt
2 CONC y-six. He was conferred the posthumous title of senior mentor (''taifu'' 太傅). Xie An is often referred to as Xie fu 謝傅 or Tutor Xie. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie An was a learned scholar. The ''Shishuo xinyu'' records numerous ancedotes about him, and in many of them he displays unusual erudtion and wit. In one especially notable passage, Xie An debates with younger members of his fami
2 CONC ly which lines of the ''Mao shi'' are the best. In his youth he also discussed the famous logical conundrum, “a white horse is not a horse.” 
2 CONT
2 CONT The monograph on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' lists Xie An's collected works in ten ''juan''. The two Tang histories record a collection in five ''juan''. This was lost in the Song. Yan Kejun has collected seven of his prose wr
2 CONC itings in ''Quan shanggu Qin Han Sanguo Liuchao wen''. Lu Qinli includes three of his poems in ''Xian Qin Han Wei Jin Nanbaichao shi'', two of which he composed for the Lan ting gathering of 353.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
