1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Xie Kun 謝鯤 (282–324), ''zi'' Youyu 幼輿. Eastern Jin literatus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Kun was the son of Xie Heng 謝衡, who served as chancellor of the national university during the Western Jin. His grandfather Xie Zuan 謝纉 held the office of leader of court gentlemen of the agricultural office. During his youth,
2 CONC  Xie Kun associated with the group of eccentrics known as the “Ba da” 八達 or “Eight Untrammeled Ones.” Xie Kun did not observe ritual decorum, was fond of the ''Laozi'' and ''Zhuangzi'', and was a skilled singer and zither player. 
2 CONC He also admired the Seven Worthies of the Bamboo Grove. His nephew Xie An 謝安 (320–385) said about him, “If he had met the Seven Worthies, they would have certainly taken him by the arm and led into the Bamboo Grove” (''Shishuo xin
2 CONC yu'' 8/97). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Kun briefly served as an aide to the Prince of Donghai 東海, Sima Yue 司馬越 (d. 311), who dominated the Western Jin court until the fall of Luoyang in 311. He resigned on grounds of illness when he ascertained that Sima Yue was ab
2 CONC out to be overthrown. He went to the south where he became senior administrator to Wang Dun 王敦 (265–324). For his success in putting down the revolt of Du Tao 杜弢 in Sichuan, he was enfeoffed as Marquis of Xian Commune 賢亭. He resig
2 CONC ned from office when his mother died, but returned to the position of senior administrator under Wang Dun. He tried to dissuade Wang from his plan to invade the capital. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After Xie Kun died in 324, Emperor Ming 明 (r. 323–324), who put down Wang Dun's insurrection, honored Kun with the posthumous position of ''taichang'' 太常 (chamberlain for ceremonials) and the posthumous title of Marquis of Kang 康.
2 CONC  Xie Kun had a younger brother, Xie Pou 謝裒, who was the father of Xie An. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The monograph on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' lists Xie Kun's collected works in six ''juan'' and cites a Liang period catalogue that records a collection in two ''juan''. The ''Jiu Tang shu'' records a collection of the same s
2 CONC ize. This was lost before the Song. None of his writings has survived.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Ding Fulin 丁福林. Dong Jin Nanchao de Xieshi wenxue jituan 東晉南朝的謝氏文學集團, 1–4. Ha'erbin: Heilongjiang jiaoyu chubanshe, 1998.
2 CONT
