1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Xie Hongwei 謝弘微 (392–433). Late Eastern Jin and Liu-Song period literatus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Hongwei's original name was Mi 密, but in order to avoid a taboo on his stepmother's name, he was called by his ''zi'' Hongwei. His ancestral home was Yangjia 陽夏 in Chen 陳 commandery (modern Taikang 太康, Henan). He was the son o
2 CONC f Xie En 謝恩 and the younger brother of Xie Yao 謝曜 (d. 427). Because his paternal uncle Xie Jun 謝峻 (d. 400) had no male heirs, in 402, Xie Hongwei was designated as his heir, and in 405, he inherited Xie Jun's title of Marquis of J
2 CONC ianchang 建昌. Xie Jun was quite wealthy and owned large landed estates and a thousand slaves and servants. All that Xie Hongwei took as his inheritance was several thousand scrolls of books. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Jun's younger brother Xie Hun 謝混 (381?–412) especially admired Xie Hongwei and even referred to him as Master Wei 微子. Xie Hun was the leading figure of a Xie family literary group that included Xie Lingyun 謝靈運 (385–433), Xie H
2 CONC ongwei, Xie Zhan 謝瞻 (383?–421), Xie Hui 謝晦 (390–426), and Xie Yao. They gathered periodically in the family home located in the Wuyi xiang 烏衣巷 district of the capital Jiankang. This group was called the “Wuyi associates” 烏衣之遊. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Hongwei began his official career as supernumerary gentleman cavalier attendant, followed by an assignment as aide to Sima Dewen 司馬德文,  Prince of Langye, the future Emperor Gong (385–421, r. 418–420). In 412, Xie Hun was kille
2 CONC d in a factional struggle, and Xie Hun's wife, the Princess of Jinling 晉陵 (d. 432), the daughter of Emperor Xiaowu 孝武  (r. 373–396), was ordered to leave the Xie family and remarry. Xie Hun had no sons, and the princess entrusted 
2 CONC all of Xie Hun's affairs to Xie Hongwei. Xie Hun was quite wealthy. Xie Hongwei meticulously accounted for all of Xie Hun's possessions and even increased the value of his estate. In 421, when Liu Yu 劉裕 (356–422) founded the Song 
2 CONC dynasty, the princess was demoted to Lady of Dongxiang 東鄉 and allowed to return to the Xie family. After finding that all of the family possessions were in good order, she exlaimed, “His excellency [Xie Hun] always admired this so
2 CONC n. This can be regarded as knowing a person. His excellency has not really died.” When the princess died in 432, Xie Hun's fortune was to be given to Xie Hongwei. However, Xie Hongwei refused to accept any of it. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the founding of the Song, Xie Hongwei was assigned as instructor to Liu Yu's third son Liu Yilong 劉義隆 (407–453), the future Emperor Wen (r. 424–453), who at that time was serving as regional inspector of Jingzhou. When Liu Y
2 CONC ilong acceded to the imperial throne in 424, he appointed Xie Hongwei gentleman of the palace gate, and later promoted him to vice director of the Ministry of Personnel. For the next few years, he along with Wang Tanshou 王曇首 (394–
2 CONC 430), Wang Hua 王華 (385–427), Yin Jingren 殷景仁 (390–440, and Liu Zhan 劉湛 (392–440) were the principal advisers to the emperor. They were known as the “Five Courtiers” (Wuchen 五臣). In handling official matters, Xie Hongwei was discre
2 CONC et and prudent and did not utter criticisms of people. After presenting a petition to the court, he burned the draft copy. His contemporaries compared him to Kong Guang 孔光 (65 b.c.e.–5 c.e.) of the Former Han. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Hongwei died in 433 at the age of forty-two. He was a skilled writer. The monographs on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' and the two Tang histories list his collected works in two ''juan''. None of his writings survives. His so
2 CONC n Xie Zhuang 謝莊 (421–466) was a famous writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng and Shen Yucheng, Zhongguo wenxuejia dacidian, 445. 
2 CONT * Ding Fulin 丁福林. Dong Jin Nanchao de Xieshi wenxue jituan 東晉南朝的謝氏文學集團, 67–69. Harbin: Heilongjiang jiaoyu chubanshe, 1998.
2 CONT
