1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Xie Shang 謝尚 (308–357), ''zi'' Renzu 仁祖. Eastern Jin literatus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Xie Shang's ancestral home was Yangjia 陽夏 in Chen 陳 commandery (modern Taikang 太康, Henan). He was the eldest son of Xie Kun 謝鯤 (282–324). His boyhood ''zi'' was Jianshi 堅石. He succeeded to his father's title as Marquis of Xian Com
2 CONC mune. Ca. 324, Wang Dao 王導 (276–339) appointed Xie Shang to his staff. He considered Xie Shang comparable to Wang Rong 王戎 (234–305) and referred to him as the “younger Anfeng.” (Wang Rong had the title of Marquis of Anfeng.) Wang 
2 CONC Dao had heard that Xie was a skilled dancer, and he asked him to perform the Mynabird Dance. Donning a robe and head cloth, Xie began to dance in a free and uninhibited manner. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ca. 329, Xie Shang was assigned as companion to the Prince of Guiji, Sima Yu 司馬昱 (320–372) and also appointed palace attendant. He later was sent out as governor of Liyang 歷陽 (administrative seat, modern He 和 county, Anhui), and a
2 CONC dministrator of Jiangxia 江夏 (modern Ezhou 鄂州, Hebei). In 334, Yu Liang 庾亮 (289–340) was assigned as regional inspector of Jingzhou 荊州 (administrative seat, Jiangling) in which Jiangxia was located. Yu Liang and Xie Shang began to 
2 CONC revise the court music. However, Yu Liang died before the project was completed. In 343, Xie Shang was given the title of leader of court gentlemen. He subsequently was assigned as governor of Xuancheng 宣城 (modern Xuancheng, Anhui
2 CONC ). 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 344, Yu Bing 庾冰 (296–344), who had replaced his elder brother Yu Liang as regional inspector of Jiangzhou 江州 (modern Hubei and Jiangxi), died. Xie Shang was appointed to replace him. Soon thereafter he was assigned as regional 
2 CONC inspector of Yuzhou 豫州 (modern Anhui) with his headquarters in Liyang. About this time Xie Shang was riding a boat in the Niuzhu 牛渚 area (northeast of modern Dangtu 當涂, Anhui). He heard Yuan Hong 袁宏 (328–376) reciting a poem on a 
2 CONC historical theme. He invited Yuan to join him on his boat and praised Yuan's piece. From this time on, Yuan, who had been making a living transporting funds from land taxes, became quite well known. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 348, Huan Wen 桓溫 (312–373) launched a military expedition against the north. He ordered Xie Shang to lead a force to Shouchun 壽春  (modern Shou county, Anui). In 352, Xie Shang accompanied Yin Hao 殷浩 on a northern campaign. His 
2 CONC army was routed by Zhang Yu 張遇  (d. 353) at Xuchang 許昌. Xie Shang escaped punishment by the intervention of Chu Suanzi 褚蒜子 (324–384), empress of Emperor Kang (342–344), who held de facto authority at the Eastern Jin court. Soon th
2 CONC ereafter Xie Shang earned merit for obtaining the “imperial” seals of the Ran-Wei state led by Ran Min 冉閔 (d. 352). He was recalled to the Jin court where he was appointed supervising censor and assigned to guard the fortress at S
2 CONC hitou 石頭. In 353, Xie Shang was named vice director of the Department of State Affairs and again assigned as regional inspector of Yuzhou. In 355, he was appointed General Who Defends the West with his headquarters in the strategi
2 CONC c city of Shouyang 壽陽 (administrative seat, modern Shou county, Anhui). While in Shouyang, Xie Shang recruited a troupe of musicians. He also had them construct a bell and stone chime set. This was the first performance of bell an
2 CONC d lithophone music in the Eastern Jin. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In September 356, Huan Wen led a Jin army in a successful campaign to recover Luoyang. He proposed to transfer the capital from Jiankang to Luoyang. He petitioned the court to have Xie Shang take charge of military affairs in the 
2 CONC Luoyang district. However, he was too ill to take up the post. Xie Shang died at the age of fifty in 357 in Liyang. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The monograph on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' mentions that a Liang period catalogue listed Xie Shang's collected works in ten ''juan''. This was lost before the Tang. Both Tang histories record a collection in five ''juan''. T
2 CONC his was lost in the Song. Lu Qinli preserves three of Xie Shang's poems in ''Xian Qin Han Wei Jin Nanbeichao shi''. Yan Kejun has collected four of his prose pieces in ''Quan shanggu Sandai Qin Han Sanguo Liuchao wen''.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng and Shen Yucheng, ''Zhongguo wenxuejia dacidian'', 449–50. 
2 CONT * Ding Fulin 丁福林. ''Dong Jin Nanbeichao de Xieshi wenxue jituan'' 東晉南北朝的謝氏文學集團, 21–26. Harbin: Heilongjiang jiaoyu chubanshe, 1999.
2 CONT
2 CONT Works
2 CONT
2 CONT “Dadao qu” 大道曲 (Song of the grand avenue)
2 CONT
2 CONT Translation
2 CONT
2 CONT * Hsieh, Daniel. ''The Evolution of Jueju Verse'', 126–27. New York: Peter Lang, 1996.
2 CONT
